
  [1mApproach B — Separate session store beneath auth[0m
  [38;5;153msession.js[39m is a storage-agnostic identity store; [38;5;153mauth.js[39m calls into it. Swapping [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m for [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m or a
  cookie later touches one file that knows nothing about login.
  [3mTrades:[0m the cleanest seam at exactly the point we flagged as likely to move. Costs a second module and an indirection
  earning nothing today. Wins if you expect the persistence decision to change soon.
  [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mApproach C — Explicit session object, no module state[0m
  [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns a [38;5;153mSession[39m object; [38;5;153mloadSession()[39m rehydrates from [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m at startup; consumers take the session as
  an argument.
  [3mTrades:[0m no hidden global state and trivially testable — and it's the one that literally gives you a parameter, just on
  the consumers rather than on [38;5;153mlogin[39m. Costs threading the object through every future form, which is real friction at
  the scale you described. Wins when you need multiple concurrent identities or strict test isolation.
  [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mI recommend A[0m, with the [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m reads and writes isolated inside [38;5;153mauth.js[39m so that B is a later extraction rather
  than a rewrite. B's seam is worth having eventually; it isn't worth a second module before a second storage backend
  exists. C's testability argument is weakened by there being no test runner here at all — which is the next thing I
  need to ask about.

